  "effRoof": "What Limits Our Computing Speed?\n\nA processor has to load data and do calculations. Depending on the job, either one can be the bottleneck. Roofline analysis tells us which one — for a given computation, on given hardware.\n\n𝐴\n𝐼\n=\n𝐹\no\np\ns\n𝑀\nb\ny\nt\ne\ns\nAI=\nM\nbytes\n\t​\n\nF\nops\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n\nHow much computing happens per byte of data moved.\n\n𝑃\nr\no\no\nf\n(\n𝐴\n𝐼\n)\n=\nmin\n⁡\n(\n𝑃\np\ne\na\nk\n,\n \n𝐴\n𝐼\n×\n𝐵\np\ne\na\nk\n)\nP\nroof\n\t​\n\n(AI)=min(P\npeak\n\t​\n\n, AI×B\npeak\n\t​\n\n)\n\nThe fastest this computation could theoretically run.\n\n𝐴\n𝐼\nr\ni\nd\ng\ne\n=\n𝑃\np\ne\na\nk\n𝐵\np\ne\na\nk\nAI\nridge\n\t​\n\n=\nB\npeak\n\t​\n\nP\npeak\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n\nWhere the two limits cross over.\n\nSYMBOL\tMEANING\tUNIT\n\n𝐹\no\np\ns\nF\nops\n\t​\n\n\tFloating-point operations\tFLOPs\n\n𝑀\nb\ny\nt\ne\ns\nM\nbytes\n\t​\n\n\tData transferred\tBytes\n\n𝐴\n𝐼\nAI\tArithmetic intensity\tFLOPs/Byte\n\n𝑃\np\ne\na\nk\nP\npeak\n\t​\n\n\tPeak compute throughput\tTFLOP/s\n\n𝐵\np\ne\na\nk\nB\npeak\n\t​\n\n\tPeak memory bandwidth\tTB/s or GB/s\n\n𝐴\n𝐼\nr\ni\nd\ng\ne\nAI\nridge\n\t​\n\n\tArithmetic intensity at the ridge point\tFLOPs/Byte\nTokens Processed Together: 1,024\nAI ≈ 552.3 · Compute-bound\nMemory-bound\nCompute-bound\nRidge Point\nlow\nhigh\nArithmetic Intensity (FLOPs / Byte)\nPerformance\nDecode\nBatch\nDecode — 1 Token\nFLOPs\n96.99 MFLOPs\nEst. Memory Traffic\n97.07 MB\nAI\n1 FLOPs/Byte\nMemory-bound under the illustrative hardware configuration\nBatch — 1024 Tokens\nFLOPs\n99.32 GFLOPs\nEst. Memory Traffic\n179.83 MB\nAI\n552.3 FLOPs/Byte\nCompute-bound under the illustrative hardware configuration\n\nWhy is Batch different? Because many tokens reuse the same weights, so more computation happens per byte of data transferred.\n\nShow Calculation\nAdvanced Settings",
